<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> took place in 1806, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Emperor Francis II</a> abdicated the imperial title following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s victories and the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>. It brought an end to an institution that had existed for about a thousand years and opened the way to the reorganisation of the German lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-holy-roman-empire.jpg" alt="Dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Holy_Roman_Empire_1789.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Since the Middle Ages Western Europeans had regarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> as the legitimate continuation of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, its emperors having been proclaimed Roman emperors by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, and the emperors claimed a universal authority over Christendom. The rise of sovereign territorial states in the 16th and 17th centuries undermined that claim. By the 18th century contemporaries described the empire as irregular and sick: it had no standing army, no central treasury and an elective monarch with little central control. Samuel von Pufendorf called it a monstrosity. Most still believed it could be revived, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> passed the Imperial Recess as late as 1803.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The empire held its own in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> left the war to attend to the Second and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partitions</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> took the title Emperor of the French in 1804, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> answered on 11 August 1804 by proclaiming himself Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as well, a hereditary title meant to keep parity with other rulers. After the defeat at Austerlitz on 2 December 1805, the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> of 26 December granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and Württemberg full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> while leaving them nominally within the empire.</p><p>On 12 July 1806 those three states and thirteen lesser princes formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, a French satellite. Karl Theodor Dalberg resigned as archchancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 31 July, and on 1 August a French envoy told the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> no longer recognised the empire. On 6 August Francis II abdicated and released all imperial states and officials from their obligations. The step was meant above all to prevent Napoleon, drawn to the legacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, from claiming the imperial title and making Francis his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Reactions ranged from indifference to despair. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>'s people were horrified, and the Bavarian envoy Rechberg said he was furious at having signed away the German name. Legal commentators held the abdication lawful but doubted the emperor's power to dissolve the empire, and some subjects refused to accept its end: as late as October 1806 farmers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> thought the news a plot by their local authorities. Many Germans compared the fall to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>.</p><p>The title and the idea of the empire were never formally abolished, and the Savoyard kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> styled themselves Perpetual Vicar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> until 1946. The imperial regalia remained with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and are kept in the Imperial Treasury at the Hofburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and the Austrian emperors went on acting as protectors of the Catholic Church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
